# Line endings.
#
# Text is stored LF in the repository and checked out LF, whatever the machine
# says. This matters here because Git for Windows ships core.autocrlf=true in
# C:/Program Files/Git/etc/gitconfig; .git/config sets false, but a local
# setting cannot undo a working tree that was already written CRLF by an earlier
# checkout — and that is how the problem below survived unnoticed.
#
# What it fixed, measured 2026-08-21:
#
#   src/shared/js/runtime/runtime.bundle.js is a concatenation of the fragments
#   beside it (tools/build-unit-runtime-bundle.js, `parts.join('\n')`). The
#   fragments sat on disk as CRLF while their committed blobs were LF, so every
#   rebuild wrote a CRLF bundle and `git diff` reported 16,158 insertions and
#   16,158 deletions with ZERO content change — CRLF-normalised, the rebuilt file
#   was byte-identical to HEAD.
#
#   Git never flagged the fragments, which is why it lasted: the index held each
#   CRLF file's stat (speak.js: 87,188 bytes) against its LF blob (84,547), so a
#   refresh compared stat, matched, and never re-hashed. They read as clean while
#   `git show HEAD:… | cmp -` put the first difference at byte 3 of line 1.
#
#   It matters beyond tidiness: CLAUDE.md requires a changed fragment and the
#   regenerated bundle to be committed together, and a one-line fragment change
#   is invisible inside a 16,158-line line-ending diff.
#
# The sweep that came with this rule touched 226 files — 136 .vtt, 77 .json,
# 9 .js, 3 .py and 1 .php — every tracked blob that contained a CR, found two
# independent ways (a raw byte read of every blob, and `git grep -I`) which
# agreed exactly. Each was checked to differ from HEAD by line endings ALONE
# before it was staged, because `git add --renormalize` stages the WORKING copy:
# a file with real uncommitted edits would have had them swept into an
# "endings only" commit.
#
# An earlier note in this file said 2,455 files. That was wrong — the command
# behind it matched every file of each extension rather than the ones containing
# CR, which is visible in hindsight because its per-extension counts were the
# totals for those extensions.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary, so git never applies the rule above to them. `text=auto` would already
# spot these by their NUL bytes, but naming them saves it reading 67,943 mp3s
# and removes any doubt about a heuristic on 1.4 GB of media.
*.mp3   -text
*.mp4   -text
*.wav   -text
*.m4a   -text
*.png   -text
*.PNG   -text
*.jpg   -text
*.jpeg  -text
*.webp  -text
*.gif   -text
*.ico   -text
*.pdf   -text
*.docx  -text
*.pptx  -text
*.xlsx  -text
*.zip   -text
*.tar   -text
*.lnk   -text
*.woff2 -text
*.ttf   -text
*.otf   -text
